---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import release from '../../public/artifacts/which-candidate/2026-v3/manifest.json';
import { candidateDownload, candidateImage } from '../../scripts/prepare-which-candidate-pages.mjs';

if (release.target_year !== 2026 || !release.provisional || release.charts.length !== 9) {
	throw new Error('Review the candidate-figure release before publishing a different snapshot.');
}

const repository = 'https://github.com/hanshanley/which-candidate';
const revision = `${repository}/blob/e36e39819f7e11a606634951124557e1cf346922`;
const results = `${revision}/results/2026-v3/analysis`;
const sectionLabels: Record<string, string> = {
	'house-characteristics': 'House characteristics',
	'moderation-history': 'Moderation across elections',
	'moderation-sensitivity': 'Moderation and benchmark coverage',
	'voting-record-coverage': 'Voting-record coverage',
	'benchmark-sensitivity': 'Presidential benchmark sensitivity',
	'identity-coverage': 'Coverage and missing identities',
	'predictive-error': 'Absolute predictive error',
	'third-party-holds': 'Third parties and held comparisons',
	'source-clocks': 'Native count clocks and reporting',
};
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Which candidate?"
	description="Corrected 2026 Democratic candidate analysis: moderation, historical comparisons, voting records, uncertainty and complete downloadable queries."
	image={candidateImage(release.charts[0].files.marginoferror.png)}
>
	<section class="standard-page candidate-page">
		<header class="page-heading">
			<p class="eyebrow">2026 congressional elections</p>
			<h1>Which candidate?</h1>
			<p>Which kinds of Democrats did better than their constituencies' partisanship
				would suggest? Compare characteristics, voting records and documented
				backgrounds, keeping the limits of the evidence in view.</p>
		</header>

		<aside class="correction-note" aria-label="Correction notice">
			<strong>Corrected release v3 supersedes v2.</strong>
			This version fixes federal-incumbency coding, refreshes the counts and replaces
			unsupported Senate and identity headlines with coverage and sensitivity diagnostics.
			The <a href={`${revision}/audits/2026-v2/critical-review.md`}>original audit</a>
			and <a href={`${repository}/releases/tag/2026-v2`}>unchanged v2 archive</a> remain available.
		</aside>

		<aside class="snapshot-note" aria-label="Snapshot and interpretation">
			<strong>Acquired November 13, 2026; native count clocks November 12–13.</strong>
			These are held counts, not live election-night updates or certified final results.
			The analysis retains all 435 House and 35 Senate contests; 405 House and 28 Senate
			comparisons qualify. No primary House characteristic clears the six-test
			multiple-comparison correction. Senate samples fail the headline support gate.
		</aside>

		<div class="lead-actions">
			<a class="primary-action" href={candidateDownload(release.downloads.queries)} download>
				Complete derived queries
			</a>
			<a href={candidateDownload(release.downloads.substack)} download>Substack figure package</a>
			<a href={repository}>Code and methodology</a>
		</div>
		<p class="download-note">
			Both ZIPs include the complete derived query set, not just metadata.
			The Substack package also contains warm-paper PNG/SVG originals and captions.
			Restricted original workbooks are excluded.
			Select any figure below to open its full-size image.
		</p>

		<nav aria-label="Figures" class="figure-index">
			<ol>
				{release.charts.map((chart) => (
					<li><a href={`#${chart.id}`}>{chart.title}</a></li>
				))}
			</ol>
		</nav>

		<div class="candidate-figures">
			{release.charts.map((chart, index) => (
				<section id={chart.id} class="candidate-figure" aria-labelledby={`${chart.id}-title`}>
					<h2 id={`${chart.id}-title`}>{sectionLabels[chart.id]}</h2>
					<figure>
						<a href={candidateImage(chart.files.marginoferror.png)} data-original-chart
							aria-label={`Open full-size figure: ${chart.title}`}>
							<img src={candidateImage(chart.files.marginoferror.png)} alt={chart.alt}
								width={chart.files.marginoferror.width} height={chart.files.marginoferror.height}
								loading={index === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'} decoding="async" />
						</a>
						<figcaption>{chart.caption}</figcaption>
					</figure>
					<p class="figure-downloads">
						<a href={candidateDownload(chart.files.marginoferror.png)} data-original-chart download>
							PNG
						</a>
						<a href={candidateDownload(chart.files.marginoferror.svg)} data-original-chart download>
							Vector SVG
						</a>
						<a href={candidateDownload(chart.data)} download>Plotted values (CSV)</a>
						<a href={candidateDownload(chart.files.substack.png)} data-original-chart download>
							Substack PNG
						</a>
					</p>
				</section>
			))}
		</div>

		<section class="methods" aria-labelledby="candidate-methods">
			<h2 id="candidate-methods">How to read these comparisons</h2>
			<p>The outcome is Democratic two-party vote share minus the constituency's 2024
				presidential Democratic share. House benchmarks use the 2026 district maps;
				Senate benchmarks are statewide. One share point equals two points of
				D-minus-R margin.</p>
			<p>Association models adjust for presidential lean and lean squared. Apart from
				incumbency, primary characteristics compare verified Democratic incumbents,
				not all nominees. Ideology comes from the 118th Congress (2023–24);
				party defection uses actual votes in the 119th Congress before November 3, 2026.
				Pointwise 95% uncertainty envelopes include state-clustered and state-deletion
				uncertainty. Support and leverage gates hold sparse estimates.
				These are exploratory associations, not causal candidate effects.</p>
			<p>The Blue Dog–Congressional Progressive Caucus contrasts use state-cross-fitted
				structural residuals, not Split Ticket's exact Wins Above Replacement.
				The full 2026 contrast has only eight Blue Dogs; adding a map-qualified swing
				control leaves three. Identical-subset comparisons isolate the control from
				sample loss. This evidence establishes neither a precise moderation advantage
				nor that it shrank in 2026. Caucus rosters have different vintages; qualified
				CPC archives start in 2020, so 2016/2018 contrasts are held.</p>
			<p>Religion and race comparisons are exploratory. Census race and Hispanic-origin
				controls describe residents, not voters; Census does not collect religion.
				Old-map district controls are held where 2026 boundaries changed. Unknown
				identity is never treated as White, non-Hispanic, Protestant or atheist.</p>
			<p>Historical predictive replay compares each feature with its own same-sample baseline,
				using earlier elections and excluding test states and identified test people.
				The 2026 House absolute errors remain about 6.6–7.4 share points: small feature
				lifts do not make a calibrated forecast. Bias is observed minus predicted;
				positive means underprediction. Fixed-prediction intervals omit model-refitting
				uncertainty. This is retrospective replay, not an actual pre-election forecast.</p>
			<p>Historical evidence includes 2016, 2018, 2020, 2022 and 2024. Midterms use the
				preceding presidential election; presidential years use same-year votes.
				Cohorts, maps and roster vintages differ. An apparent coefficient difference
				is not a formal test that an association changed.</p>
			<p>Select any figure to open a full-size image. All figures, captions, methods and
				downloads remain usable without JavaScript.</p>
			<p><a href={`${results}/report.md`}>Read the frozen statistical report</a>
				&middot; <a href={`${revision}/docs/CORRECTIONS_2026_V3.md`}>Correction methods and evidence limits</a>
				&middot; <a href={candidateDownload('manifest.json')}>Checksums and provenance</a></p>
		</section>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.snapshot-note, .correction-note {
		max-width: 850px;
		padding: 1.1rem 0;
		border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.download-note, .methods p { max-width: 850px; line-height: 1.65; }
	.download-note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }
	.figure-index { margin: 2rem 0 3rem; }
	.figure-index ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; line-height: 1.8; }
	.candidate-figure { margin: 0 0 3.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 2rem; }
	.candidate-figure h2, .methods h2 {
		font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
		font-weight: 500;
		line-height: 1.2;
	}
	.candidate-figure figure { margin: 0; }
	.candidate-figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
	.candidate-figure figcaption {
		max-width: 900px;
		margin-top: 1rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
	}
	.figure-downloads { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
	.methods { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1rem; }
</style>
